00:04:26.560 --> 00:04:30.600
These results perfectly illustrate the fee and behavior drag we are about to explore.

00:04:31.000 --> 00:04:35.760
Imagine you received a $3,000 tax refund yesterday and split it evenly between two ideas.

00:04:36.360 --> 00:04:42.620
Half went into a 0.03% management expense ratio S and P500 exchange traded fund.

00:04:43.040 --> 00:04:47.640
The other half went into an actively managed energy transition fund riding the current oil

00:04:47.640 --> 00:04:55.460
surge narrative. That active fund carried a 1.15% management expense ratio plus 0.45% in trading

00:04:55.460 --> 00:05:02.300
costs. Over 15 years, the fee gap alone destroys roughly 22% of the compounded return even before

00:05:02.300 --> 00:05:09.140
any manager under performance. Spiva data shows approximately 88% of active equity managers

00:05:09.140 --> 00:05:15.260
underperformed their benchmark over 15 years net of fees. The mechanism is pure compounding

00:05:16.060 --> 00:05:21.960
higher costs create a permanent 1-2% annual headwind the manager must overcome just to match

00:05:21.960 --> 00:05:27.540
the index. What catches most people is that the decision to go active is often triggered by

00:05:27.540 --> 00:05:33.820
recency bias right after headlines like today's oil spike. You chase the theme at exactly the moment

00:05:33.820 --> 00:05:39.600
institutions may already be rotating out at higher costs. The core rule is to keep at least 70%

00:05:39.600 --> 00:05:45.700
of your TFSA and RRSP in passive indexing use thematic satellites for no more than 30%

00:05:45.700 --> 00:05:50.400
and only where you can document a clear verifiable edge that exceeds the feed drag.

